\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The game, the rescaling and the corpus.} (\textbf  {a}) The stage game of the frontier arm at $\lambda = 1$. Cells are penalties the agent is told to minimise, so $T<R<P<S$ and Option A is the dominant, defecting action. Multiplying every cell by $\lambda > 0$ is the multiplicative half of a positive affine transformation of a von Neumann--Morgenstern utility, and leaves every listed quantity where it was. The open-weight arm uses the same matrix with $P = 8$, which is fear 0.20 rather than 0.40. (\textbf  {b}) What the rescaling does change: the four cell values as the prompt prints them, at each of the six scales of the open-weight arm, with the three-level notation regime of \S  \ref {sec:regimes} on the right. Greed and fear are identical on all six rows. (\textbf  {c}) The corpus. Both arms are balanced at 80 agent-games in every model $\times $ language $\times $ scale cell.}}{7}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:design}{{1}{7}{\textbf {The game, the rescaling and the corpus.} (\textbf {a}) The stage game of the frontier arm at $\lam = 1$. Cells are penalties the agent is told to minimise, so $T<R<P<S$ and Option A is the dominant, defecting action. Multiplying every cell by $\lam > 0$ is the multiplicative half of a positive affine transformation of a von Neumann--Morgenstern utility, and leaves every listed quantity where it was. The open-weight arm uses the same matrix with $P = 8$, which is fear 0.20 rather than 0.40. (\textbf {b}) What the rescaling does change: the four cell values as the prompt prints them, at each of the six scales of the open-weight arm, with the three-level notation regime of \S \ref {sec:regimes} on the right. Greed and fear are identical on all six rows. (\textbf {c}) The corpus. Both arms are balanced at 80 agent-games in every model $\times $ language $\times $ scale cell}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.2}Magnitude or notation}{7}{subsection.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:notation}{{3.2}{7}{Magnitude or notation}{subsection.3.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cooperation against the payoff scale.} (\textbf  {a}) The open-weight arm over six scales. Bands are 95\% bootstrap intervals over 2{,}000 resamples of whole dyads. The shaded strip marks the unit regime. The curve is not monotone in any of the three models: it rises to a maximum at $\lambda = 1$ or $\lambda = 10$ and falls again by $\lambda = 100$. (\textbf  {b}) The frontier arm over three scales, which sample only the rising limb. (\textbf  {c}) The invariance test. Bars give the largest shift in cooperation between two scales; ticks give the 95th percentile of a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads. The shift a theory of the game predicts is exactly zero. Six of the seven models exceed their null.}}{8}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:response}{{2}{8}{\textbf {Cooperation against the payoff scale.} (\textbf {a}) The open-weight arm over six scales. Bands are 95\% bootstrap intervals over 2{,}000 resamples of whole dyads. The shaded strip marks the unit regime. The curve is not monotone in any of the three models: it rises to a maximum at $\lam = 1$ or $\lam = 10$ and falls again by $\lam = 100$. (\textbf {b}) The frontier arm over three scales, which sample only the rising limb. (\textbf {c}) The invariance test. Bars give the largest shift in cooperation between two scales; ticks give the 95th percentile of a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads. The shift a theory of the game predicts is exactly zero. Six of the seven models exceed their null}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}Which model looks more cooperative depends on the units}{8}{subsection.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:benchmark}{{3.3}{8}{Which model looks more cooperative depends on the units}{subsection.3.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the response is a function of.} (\textbf  {a}) Cooperation by notation regime, pooled within each arm. Whiskers are 95\% dyad bootstrap intervals. The frontier arm has no large regime, because its sweep stops at $\lambda = 10$. (\textbf  {b}) The description ladder for the open-weight arm. Bars give the BIC penalty each specification pays relative to the best one, with model, language and both personas controlled in every row. A three-level factor built from how the cells are printed beats every polynomial in $\log _{10}\lambda $ and beats the factor saturated in $\lambda $, which has three more parameters. (\textbf  {c}) Cooperation by model across the sweep, on an evenly spaced scale index. Qwen3 8B cooperates more than Gemma 3 12B at $\lambda = 0.01$ and less at every larger scale, so which of the two is the more cooperative model is a statement about the units.}}{9}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:notation}{{3}{9}{\textbf {What the response is a function of.} (\textbf {a}) Cooperation by notation regime, pooled within each arm. Whiskers are 95\% dyad bootstrap intervals. The frontier arm has no large regime, because its sweep stops at $\lam = 10$. (\textbf {b}) The description ladder for the open-weight arm. Bars give the BIC penalty each specification pays relative to the best one, with model, language and both personas controlled in every row. A three-level factor built from how the cells are printed beats every polynomial in $\log _{10}\lam $ and beats the factor saturated in $\lam $, which has three more parameters. (\textbf {c}) Cooperation by model across the sweep, on an evenly spaced scale index. Qwen3 8B cooperates more than Gemma 3 12B at $\lam = 0.01$ and less at every larger scale, so which of the two is the more cooperative model is a statement about the units}{figure.caption.3}{}}
