\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Each strategy label against the payoff scale.} Shares are of all agent-games in the arm at that scale. ``Swing'' is the largest difference between two scales, tested against a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads, exactly as in table~\ref {tab:sensitivity}. The predicted swing is zero on every row. The two unconditional rules move by three to eleven times what the two conditional ones do, and win-stay lose-shift over six scales is the one label whose movement does not clear its null. ``Ambiguous'' is not a strategy: it records that several rules reproduce the run of play equally well, so its row measures how nameable the play is rather than what was played.}}{14}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:strategy}{{2}{14}{\textbf {Each strategy label against the payoff scale.} Shares are of all agent-games in the arm at that scale. ``Swing'' is the largest difference between two scales, tested against a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads, exactly as in table~\ref {tab:sensitivity}. The predicted swing is zero on every row. The two unconditional rules move by three to eleven times what the two conditional ones do, and win-stay lose-shift over six scales is the one label whose movement does not clear its null. ``Ambiguous'' is not a strategy: it records that several rules reproduce the run of play equally well, so its row measures how nameable the play is rather than what was played}{table.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Discussion}{14}{section.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.1}A cooperation rate is a statement about one choice of units}{14}{subsection.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Scale sensitivity per model.} ``Swing'' is the largest difference in cooperation rate between two payoff scales and ``at'' names the scale where cooperation peaks. ``Monotone'' records whether the response increases or decreases throughout the sweep. ``Coop. shift'' repeats the swing as a test against a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads, and ``mix shift'' is the same test on the largest total-variation distance between the strategy mixes at two scales, reported over the five categories the read-out emits and again over the four named rules alone, on which movement into the ambiguous category cannot contribute. ``Best description'' is the specification of table~\ref {tab:ladder} with the lowest BIC for that model. The predicted value of every shift column is exactly zero, because a positive rescaling of a von Neumann--Morgenstern utility leaves the game unchanged. Nothing in this table is comparable across the two arms, whose horizon, base matrix and decoding settings all differ (\S  \ref {sec:confounds}).}}{15}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sensitivity}{{3}{15}{\textbf {Scale sensitivity per model.} ``Swing'' is the largest difference in cooperation rate between two payoff scales and ``at'' names the scale where cooperation peaks. ``Monotone'' records whether the response increases or decreases throughout the sweep. ``Coop. shift'' repeats the swing as a test against a permutation null that shuffles the scale label across whole dyads, and ``mix shift'' is the same test on the largest total-variation distance between the strategy mixes at two scales, reported over the five categories the read-out emits and again over the four named rules alone, on which movement into the ambiguous category cannot contribute. ``Best description'' is the specification of table~\ref {tab:ladder} with the lowest BIC for that model. The predicted value of every shift column is exactly zero, because a positive rescaling of a von Neumann--Morgenstern utility leaves the game unchanged. Nothing in this table is comparable across the two arms, whose horizon, base matrix and decoding settings all differ (\S \ref {sec:confounds})}{table.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}Notation, and what the account can and cannot claim}{15}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The description ladder.} Every specification predicts the cooperation rate of an agent-game from the same controls for model, prompt language, own persona and opponent persona, and differs only in how the payoff scale enters. $\Delta $BIC is measured from the best specification within each arm; $k$ counts the free parameters the specification adds to the controls. The saturated row is the ceiling on what any function of $\lambda $ can fit, so a row that matches it at lower $k$ has found the shape of the response. In the frontier arm only two notation regimes are present, so the fractional flag and the regime factor are the same specification, and a cubic in $\log _{10}\lambda $ is collinear with a quadratic over three scales.}}{16}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ladder}{{4}{16}{\textbf {The description ladder.} Every specification predicts the cooperation rate of an agent-game from the same controls for model, prompt language, own persona and opponent persona, and differs only in how the payoff scale enters. $\Delta $BIC is measured from the best specification within each arm; $k$ counts the free parameters the specification adds to the controls. The saturated row is the ceiling on what any function of $\lam $ can fit, so a row that matches it at lower $k$ has found the shape of the response. In the frontier arm only two notation regimes are present, so the fractional flag and the regime factor are the same specification, and a cubic in $\log _{10}\lam $ is collinear with a quadratic over three scales}{table.caption.9}{}}
